<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.6.1: Finding and Reasoning Unknown Factors</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02177</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.6.1: Finding and Reasoning Unknown Factors</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5c1ddb87-cc96-4a09-b38a-ff20c85f3057</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Examine the two puzzles that involve side lengths and areas of rectangles. Can you find the missing area in Figure A and the missing length in Figure B? Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
<para>Figure A</para>
<image alt="" class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="228" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/355b0485397841d8c7f72ee2ea727afedd16f156" width="300"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the missing area in Figure A above? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the missing area.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>To determine the area of the blue rectangle, we need to figure out it’s width and length. </para>
    <para>The top shape is a square measuring 8 in. x 8 in.  If the measurement of the longer left side is 10 inches, then the width of the blue rectangle can be an equation. </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}8 + W &amp;=&amp; 10 \\ W &amp;=&amp; 2  \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>The width of the rectangle is 2 inches. </para>
    <para>Because the bottom of the square is 8 inches, the length of the blue rectangle can be written as an equation.  But we have an unknown \(z\).   </para>
    <para>\(L = 8 + z\)</para>
    <para>If we look at the measurements of the smaller square, we see that we can use an equation to calculate \(z\). </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}z + 3 &amp;=&amp; 5 \\ z &amp;=&amp; 2 \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>Now, we can substitute the 2 for \(z\) in the length equation. </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}L &amp;=&amp; 8 + 2 \\   L &amp;=&amp; 10 \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>The area of the blue rectangle is \(8 x 10 \)to equal 80 square inches.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<para>Figure B</para>
<image height="312" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f37442dd8e0f8df3ef09997896a457990f35d249" width="360"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the missing length in Figure B above? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the missing length.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>You have to start with what you know and can figure out the other sides. </para>
    <para>Sides of orange rectangle </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}L \cdot W  &amp;=&amp; Area \\ 12W &amp;=&amp; 60  \\ W &amp;=&amp; 5 \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>Sides of green rectangle </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}L \cdot W  &amp;= &amp;Area  \\ (5+3) \cdot W &amp;=&amp; 48 \\ 8w &amp;=&amp; 48 \\ w &amp;=&amp; 6 \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>Sides of combined rectangles </para>
    <para>The measurement of the long side of combined orange and green rectangle will be equal.  This helps figure out one side of the blue rectangle. </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12 + 6 &amp;=&amp; 3 + w + 11 \\ 18&amp; = &amp;w + 14 \\ w &amp;= &amp;4 \end{array}\)</para>
    <para>Sides of blue rectangle </para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}L \cdot W&amp;  =&amp; Area \\ L\cdot 4 &amp;=&amp; 36  \\ L &amp;= &amp;9\end{array}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
